{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Trigonometric.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks.three_dvd_becomes_infeasible","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks.three_dvd_becomes_infeasible","kind":"theorem","line":170,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ThreeDividesKBreaks.lean#L170-L181","doc":"**The multiples of 3 fail too, just later.** `cos (k·δ) < 0` once the phase\npasses `π/2`, so the `3 ∣ k` class is not permanently feasible either — at the\npin the first casualty is `k = 63`. Kept so a reader does not conclude that only\none direction of the equivalence breaks. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks.not_forall_feasible_iff_three_dvd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks.not_forall_feasible_iff_three_dvd","kind":"theorem","line":160,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ThreeDividesKBreaks.lean#L160-L168","doc":"**So no `θ` with `δ > 0` can satisfy `χ_k ≥ 0 ↔ 3 ∣ k` for all `k`**, once\none such `k` is in the window. Stated as the refutation of the universal claim,\nwhich is the form the open item was posed in. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks.pattern_fails_at_residue_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks.pattern_fails_at_residue_two","kind":"theorem","line":138,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ThreeDividesKBreaks.lean#L138-L158","doc":"**The pattern fails: a `k ≡ 2 (mod 3)` whose phase has reached the window is\nfeasible.**\n\n`3 ∤ k` and `χ_k ≥ 0` together contradict `χ_k ≥ 0 ↔ 3 ∣ k`. So this exhibits\nthe failure from the hypothesis that such a `k` exists; the companion probe\nsupplies `k = 23` at the proton pin, where `δ = 0.0254231…` puts `k·δ = 0.5847`\ninside `[π/6, 5π/6] = [0.5236, 2.6180]`.\n\nNote what the hypothesis costs: only that the accumulated phase has entered a\nwindow of width `2π/3`. Since consecutive members of this residue class step the\nphase by `3δ`, any `δ ≤ 2π/9` cannot step over the window — so such a `k` exists\nfor every small positive `δ`. That existence argument is NOT formalised here\n(see the file header); this theorem is the implication it feeds. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks.cos_four_pi_thirds_add_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks.cos_four_pi_thirds_add_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ThreeDividesKBreaks.lean#L123-L136","doc":"**The feasibility window for the `2 mod 3` class.**\n\n`cos (4π/3 + φ) ≥ 0` exactly when the total angle has come round to the fourth\nquadrant, i.e. `φ ∈ [π/6, 5π/6]`: at `φ = π/6` the angle is `3π/2` and the\ncosine is `0`, at `φ = 5π/6` it is `2π + π/6`. So a `2 mod 3` candidate becomes\nFEASIBLE once its accumulated phase reaches `π/6` — it does not need to reach a\nhalf turn. That small threshold is why the pattern breaks in this class first. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks.cos_of_three_mul_add_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks.cos_of_three_mul_add_one","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ThreeDividesKBreaks.lean#L109-L119","doc":"**`k = 3m+1`: offset `2π/3`.** Included so the three classes are visibly a\npartition and a reader is not left wondering whether this one behaves\ndifferently. It does not — it has the largest threshold, `5π/6`, which is why\nthe probe finds its first feasible member at `k = 103` rather than 23. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks.cos_of_three_mul_add_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks.cos_of_three_mul_add_two","kind":"theorem","line":96,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ThreeDividesKBreaks.lean#L96-L107","doc":"**`k = 3m+2`: a fixed `4π/3` offset plus the accumulated phase.**\n\nAt `δ = 0` this is `cos (4π/3) = −1/2 < 0`, which is the infeasibility of the\nnon-multiples. The offset is fixed; the phase is not — and §3 is what happens\nwhen the phase catches up. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks.cos_of_three_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks.cos_of_three_mul","kind":"theorem","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ThreeDividesKBreaks.lean#L84-L94","doc":"**`k = 3m`: the `2π/3` part is whole turns, so only the accumulated phase\nsurvives.**\n\nThis is why the multiples of 3 are the feasible ones at `δ = 0` (`cos 0 = 1`),\nand equally why they STOP being feasible once `k·δ` passes `π/2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks.two_cos_recursion","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.ThreeDividesKBreaks.two_cos_recursion","kind":"theorem","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/ThreeDividesKBreaks.lean#L66-L79","doc":"**The Chebyshev recursion for `2 cos(k·θ)`.**\n\n`V` is `2×2` with `det V = 1` and `tr V = −1 − x`, so Cayley–Hamilton gives\n`χ_{k+2} = (tr V)·χ_{k+1} − χ_k`. The trace sequence `2 cos(k·θ)` satisfies the\nsame recursion with `2 cos θ = tr V`, which is what identifies the two — and it\nis why one cosine can replace the ten separate polynomial identities that\n`GammaGammaSelectionTen` carries. ","signatureChunk":0}]}